engine: a recording downloads nothing, survives a missing file, and tells no server it is leaving

A 2014 uG recording's loading section names a sv_downloadurl and a few files
the recorder's client fetched on the day (a parachute model, two screams, a
map text). Twelve years on, playing it back: the client precached the
resource list, found the model missing, and — because the server had marked
it fatal-if-missing — disconnected itself mid-parse, leaving the rest of
the packet to be read as garbage ("illegible server message 150",
"svc_bad", "overflow!", a different one each run). Now a recording treats
every resource as on hand (nowhere to download from), a missing file is
silence or an entity that draws nothing rather than a disconnect, and no
"disconnect" is sent when it stops, since there is nobody to tell.

diff -ur a/engine/client/cl_main.c b/engine/client/cl_main.c
--- a/engine/client/cl_main.c	2026-09-13 21:19:09.395864463 -0600
+++ b/engine/client/cl_main.c	2026-09-13 21:19:09.417864660 -0600
@@ -1572,6 +1572,7 @@
 	byte	data[32];
 
 	if( cls.state == ca_disconnected ) return;
+	if( cls.demoplayback ) return; // a recording has no server to tell
 
 	MSG_Init( &buf, "LastMessage", data, sizeof( data ));
 	MSG_BeginClientCmd( &buf, clc_stringcmd );
diff -ur a/engine/client/cl_parse.c b/engine/client/cl_parse.c
--- a/engine/client/cl_parse.c	2026-09-13 21:19:09.396864472 -0600
+++ b/engine/client/cl_parse.c	2026-09-13 21:19:09.417864660 -0600
@@ -481,6 +481,16 @@
 			continue;
 		}
 
+		// A recording has nowhere to download from: the server in it is gone, and its
+		// sv_downloadurl of 2014 with it. Whatever is missing is reported when the game
+		// tries to load it, as it is for every other recording; asking the web build's
+		// network for an HTTP fetch here handed the answer to the packet parser instead.
+		if( cls.demoplayback )
+		{
+			CL_MoveToOnHandList( p );
+			continue;
+		}
+
 		if( cls.state == ca_active && !cl_download_ingame.value )
 		{
 			Con_Printf( "skipping in game download of %s\n", p->szFileName );
